== T16 FFI surface-path probe ==
golden dir: Vendor/herdr/herdr-protocol/tests/fixtures/golden
probe fixtures generated in-memory (no writes needed)
created client (phase 0)
receive(golden welcome)  -> code 0 (0 = OK)
phase after welcome      -> 1
receive(golden snapshot) -> code 0 (0 = OK)
receive(PaneSurface)     -> code 14 (0 = OK, 15 = SURFACE_REJECTED)
herdr_client_surface     -> len 0 (code 0)
phase at end             -> 1
destroyed; probe complete

CONCLUSION (T16 structural finding):
herdr_client_receive(PaneSurface) = 14 (HERDR_CODE_SURFACE_REJECTED) and
herdr_client_surface stays empty even after a valid golden welcome+snapshot.
Root cause: herdr-ios-ffi never activates ClientShellState's endpoint
projection; `active` stays ClientEndpointId::Home while the endpoint is
ClientEndpointId::Ssh(ProfileId::generate()), so receive_surface's
`self.active != incoming.endpoint` check always rejects.
`activate_endpoint_projection` is pub(crate) in herdr-client-core (unreachable
from herdr-ios-ffi); only `complete_activation` + `PendingEndpointActivation`
can flip it and neither is exposed through the committed C ABI. Vendor/herdr
is read-only for T16 (task MUST NOT), so pane-surface commit through the FFI
is BLOCKED upstream of Swift.

Consequences for T16 (documented, not silently skipped):
- Chrome (workspaces/tabs/panes/focus/notifications) renders from real
  committed snapshot frames through the FFI — unaffected.
- The native pane-surface renderer is fully implemented and tested against
  surface-2x2.json — byte-identical to what herdr_client_surface will return
  once the FFI exposes endpoint activation (same serde type).
- A live v0.9.0 server WILL send PaneSurface frames; HerdrSessionModel maps
  the typed surfaceRejected error to a non-fatal surfaceUnavailable note
  (client stays Online; the frame is dropped, never half-applied) instead of
  tearing down an otherwise healthy session.
- Same gap also freezes semantic input (unfreeze happens in
  complete_activation) — blocks T17 input the same way; routing hint for the
  orchestrator: a small additive FFI surface (activate-projection /
  complete-activation + resize) owned by a Vendor-touching task.
